\section{Tuning Parameters}{

The model arguments of \pkgfun{parsnip}{bart} correspond to arguments of
\code{\link[=bartisan_control]{bartisan_control()}} as follows, and each can be tuned with
\pkgfun{tune}{tune}:
\describe{
\item{\code{trees}}{\code{num_trees}, the number of trees. Default is 50.}
\item{\code{prior_terminal_node_coef}}{\code{gamma}, the base of the probability that
a node at depth \code{d} splits, \code{gamma * (1 + d)^(-beta)}. Default is .95.}
\item{\code{prior_terminal_node_expo}}{\code{beta}, the exponent in that probability.
Default is 2.}
\item{\code{prior_outcome_range}}{\code{k}, which sets the prior standard deviation
of the forest to \code{3 / k} times the natural scale of its additive
predictor, so that larger values shrink the fit harder toward the
intercept-only model. Default is 2. Its tuning range is .5 to 5 rather
than the \pkg{dials} default, which extends to 0, where the prior is so
diffuse that the fit is of no use.}
}
Any other argument of \code{\link[=bartisan]{bartisan()}} or \code{\link[=bartisan_control]{bartisan_control()}} is an engine
argument and is supplied to \pkgfun{parsnip}{set_engine} (e.g.,
\code{set_engine("bartisan", family = gaussian(), num_draws = 400)}); these can be
tuned too.
}

\section{Families}{

The family is chosen from the outcome by the rules \code{\link[=bartisan]{bartisan()}} uses when its
\code{family} argument is left out, unless it is supplied as an engine argument. In
the \code{"regression"} mode, a numeric outcome is fit with \code{\link[=dpm]{dpm()}} by default; in
the \code{"classification"} mode, a factor with two levels is fit with \code{\link[=binomial]{binomial()}},
an ordered factor with \code{\link[=ordinal]{ordinal()}}, and any other factor with \code{\link[=multinomial]{multinomial()}};
and in the \code{"censored regression"} mode, where the outcome is a
\pkgfun{survival}{Surv} object with right censoring, the outcome is fit with
\code{\link[=dpm_aft]{dpm_aft()}}. \code{\link[=bartisan]{bartisan()}} prints a message naming the family it chose each
time a model is fit, which during resampling or tuning means once per fit;
supplying the family as an engine argument silences it. Any family whose
response is compatible with the mode can be requested this way, such as
\code{poisson()} for a count in the \code{"regression"} mode or \code{\link[=ph]{ph()}} in the
\code{"censored regression"} mode.
Note that \pkg{parsnip} evaluates engine arguments before passing them on, so a
family supplied as an object (e.g., \code{family = poisson()}) is stored in the
call of the fit in full, which makes it long to print; supplying the family by
name (e.g., \code{family = "poisson"}) gives the same model with a short call.
}

\section{Predictions}{

The following prediction types are available through
\pkgfun{parsnip}{predict.model_fit}:
\describe{
\item{\code{"numeric"}}{the posterior mean of the response (i.e.,
\code{\link[=predict.bartisan_fit]{predict.bartisan_fit()}} with \code{type = "response"}), in the \code{"regression"}
mode.}
\item{\code{"class"}}{the category with the highest posterior mean probability,
in the \code{"classification"} mode.}
\item{\code{"prob"}}{the posterior mean probability of each category, in the
\code{"classification"} mode.}
\item{\code{"conf_int"}}{a credible interval for the quantity reported by
\code{"numeric"} or for each of those reported by \code{"prob"}, in either mode.}
\item{\code{"pred_int"}}{a posterior predictive interval for the outcome, in the
\code{"regression"} mode. This requires \pkg{rstantools}.}
\item{\code{"time"}}{the posterior mean of the median survival time (i.e.,
\code{\link[=predict.bartisan_fit]{predict.bartisan_fit()}} with \code{type = "response"}), in the
\code{"censored regression"} mode.}
\item{\code{"survival"}}{the posterior mean of the survival function at the
times given in \code{eval_time}, in the \code{"censored regression"} mode. The
result has one row per row of \code{new_data}, each holding a tibble with
columns \code{.eval_time} and \code{.pred_survival}; setting
\code{interval = "confidence"} adds \code{.pred_lower} and \code{.pred_upper}, the limits
of a credible interval for the survival probability.}
\item{\code{"hazard"}}{the posterior mean of the hazard at the times given in
\code{eval_time}, in the \code{"censored regression"} mode, laid out as for
\code{"survival"} with the column \code{.pred_hazard}.}
\item{\code{"linear_pred"}}{the posterior mean of the additive predictor, in the
\code{"censored regression"} mode. This is the location of the log survival
time for the accelerated failure time families and the negative of the log
hazard ratio for \code{\link[=ph]{ph()}}, so that it increases with the survival time for
every family, as \pkg{censored} reports it; setting \code{increasing = FALSE}
reverses its sign. For \code{\link[=dpm_aft_ls]{dpm_aft_ls()}} and \code{\link[=lognormal_aft_ls]{lognormal_aft_ls()}} it is the
predictor of the location only, so it does not order the units by their
survival where their scales differ.}
\item{\code{"raw"}}{the posterior draws of the mean of the response, as a matrix
with one row per draw and one column per row of \code{new_data} (or, in the
\code{"classification"} mode, the draws of the category probabilities, as an
array with a third dimension for the categories, and in the
\code{"censored regression"} mode, the draws of the median survival time).}
}
\pkg{parsnip} allows an evaluation time of 0, at which \code{\link[=predict.bartisan_fit]{predict.bartisan_fit()}}
reports nothing. There the survival probability is reported as 1, since every
unit has survived to it, and the hazard as \code{NA}, since for most of the
families it is not finite.

The intervals, including those \code{interval = "confidence"} adds to the
\code{"survival"} predictions, are equal-tailed: their limits are the \code{(1 - level) / 2} and
\code{(1 + level) / 2} quantiles of the posterior draws, where \code{level} is the
argument of that name to \pkgfun{parsnip}{predict.model_fit} (default .95).
They are not highest posterior density intervals, which for a skewed
posterior, such as that of a probability near 0 or 1, would be narrower and
shifted toward the mode. Setting \code{std_error = TRUE} adds a \code{.std_error}
column holding the posterior standard deviation.

The underlying \verb{<bartisan_fit>} object is available with
\pkgfun{parsnip}{extract_fit_engine}, and every method in the package applies
to it, including \code{\link[=estimate_effect]{estimate_effect()}}, \code{\link[=variable_importance]{variable_importance()}}, and
\code{\link[=diagnose]{diagnose()}}.
